{
  "id": "minimum-vertices-to-reach-all-nodes",
  "questionId": "minimum-vertices-to-reach-all-nodes",
  "slug": "minimum-vertices-to-reach-all-nodes",
  "title": "Minimum Vertices to Reach All Nodes",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Graphs",
  "patterns": [
    "graph dfs/bfs",
    "directed graph"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "you don't need to simulate any traversal at all here - think about which nodes could possibly be 'discovered' by following edges from somewhere else",
    "if a node has at least one incoming edge, some other node can always reach it by first reaching that predecessor",
    "so the only nodes that must be explicit starting points are ones with zero incoming edges",
    "count incoming edges per node (in-degree), then collect every node with in-degree 0, sorted ascending"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "trying to run BFS/DFS from every node and comparing coverage, which is unnecessary and slow",
    "forgetting to sort the result in ascending order",
    "confusing in-degree with out-degree"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a directed acyclic graph with n nodes labeled 0 to n-1, described by a list of directed `edges` where each edge is `[u, v]` meaning there's an edge from u to v.\n\n**Find the smallest set of starting nodes such that every node in the graph can be reached by starting a traversal from at least one node in that set, and return the node labels in ascending order.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "2 <= n <= 10^5",
      "0 <= edges.length <= min(10^5, n * (n - 1) / 2)",
      "edges[i] = [ui, vi], 0 <= ui, vi < n, ui != vi",
      "all pairs (ui, vi) are distinct"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "no edges at all (every node is isolated)",
      "a single chain 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> ... (only node 0 is needed)",
      "multiple disconnected chains",
      "a node with several incoming edges from different sources"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 6, edges = [[0,1],[0,2],[2,5],[3,4],[4,2]]",
        "output": "[0, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 3]",
        "explanation": "from 0 you reach 1 and 2 and then 5, from 3 you reach 4 and then 2 and 5; nodes 0 and 3 have no incoming edges so both must be starting points, and together they cover everything"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 5, edges = [[0,1],[2,1],[3,1],[1,4],[2,4]]",
        "output": "[0, 2, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 2, 3]",
        "explanation": "nodes 0, 2, and 3 have no incoming edges, so all three must be included; they collectively reach 1 and 4 as well"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 3, edges = []",
        "output": "[0, 1, 2]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 1, 2]",
        "explanation": "with no edges, every node must be its own starting point"
      }
    ],
